  <!-- § 09 the cross-check. Inputs: npci-upi, payments-monthly. -->
  <section data-inputs="npci-upi, payments-monthly" data-prose="evergreen explanations and governed As-of readings">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>NPCI against the RBI</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-rbi" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 id="h-rbi" set:html={N["h-rbi"]}></h3>
            <div class="sub">Difference between NPCI's and the RBI's number of UPI payments each month, per cent of the RBI's figure, {U.rbi.from} to {U.rbi.to}.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-rbi" set:html={N["pv-rbi"]}></div><div class="c">the largest monthly gap</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-rbi" set:html={CH.rbi()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: NPCI, UPI product statistics; RBI, Payment System Indicators (DBIE report 612), from this site's payments guide. Difference computed by Measure of India.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="/guides/payments/">Payments guide</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Both count the same payments, NPCI from its switch and the RBI from what NPCI and the banks report to it, and both revise. A bar is the gap in one month. Small gaps are revisions and the timing of when a payment is booked. The RBI's figure is the one to use beside cards, NEFT and RTGS; NPCI's for anything only NPCI publishes.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {U.rbi.to}</span>
            <p>The two counts were within half a per cent in <b id="ex-rb-within" set:html={N["ex-rb-within"]}></b> of the <b id="ex-rb-n" set:html={N["ex-rb-n"]}></b> months both cover.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 misreadings -->
  <section data-inputs="npci-upi, npci-upi-apps, npci-upi-states, npci-upi-mcc" data-prose="evergreen">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"Two thousand crore payments means a billion users."</h3><p>NPCI counts payments, not people. A shopkeeper receiving a hundred payments a day is a hundred payments. NPCI publishes no count of people who use UPI.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"Maharashtra's UPI fell this month."</h3><p>State counts move with the share NPCI could not assign to any state, which rose from about one in eight to more than two in five. Compare states within a month, never a state across months.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"Groceries are a quarter of UPI spending."</h3><p>They are about a quarter of merchant payments by number, and a far smaller share of the money. Merchant categories describe the shop, not what was bought, and a shopkeeper on a personal QR code is not in the merchant table at all.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"PhonePe processed the payment."</h3><p>Apps such as PhonePe and Google Pay do not hold money or settle payments; banks do, over NPCI's network. The app table counts which app the payer opened.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"A quarter of borrowers default every month."</h3><p>A quarter of NACH debits are returned unpaid in the first attempt. Many are paid on a later attempt, and one borrower with a shortfall can bounce several debits. The rate measures strain on accounts, not defaults.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">6</span><div><h3>"A missing app or category means it had no payments."</h3><p>NPCI lists only the apps and categories above its cut-offs each month. A gap is a month it was not listed.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

